The longitudinal distribution of UPDRS scores indicates a clear increase in mean values over time, as illustrated in Figure 2.1, reflecting progressive motor impairment. While the overall trend appears approximately linear, systematic variations in the rate of increase across visits suggest a mild non-linear pattern consistent with a potential quadratic time effect. Substantial variability is observed both within and between patients, as illustrated by the divergent individual trajectories around the overall mean trend. Confidence intervals around the mean trajectory (Figure 2.2) widen over time, indicating increasing variability that may violate the assumption of homoscedasticity. This indicates heterogeneity in disease progression across individuals, as well as within-subject fluctuations over time.

The correlation matrix of UPDRS measurements across visits indicates moderate to strong positive correlations between repeated observations within individuals, confirming the presence of within-subject dependence. Correlations tend to be higher for visits closer in time and lower for more distant visits, although the pattern is not strictly monotonic. This suggests a temporally structured correlation, where measurements within the same individual are related over time. These findings motivate the use of mixed-effects models, which account for such correlation through subject-specific random effects, including both random intercepts and slopes.

## Statistical Analysis

A linear mixed effects model was fitted to examine how the mean UPDRS scores evolve over time and how this evolution is influenced by smoking status, while also accounting for age and sex. The mean structure includes Visit, a quadratic term for Visit to account for the observed acceleration over time, smoking status, the interaction between Visit and smoking status, age, and sex as fixed effects. To account for between-patient variability in both the severity at baseline and progression rates, random intercepts and slopes for Visit were included. To address the acceleration of residuals over time, an exponential variance function was applied. The error terms are assumed to be normally distributed and independent of the random effects. 

The missing data is assumed to be missing at random, which allows yielding unbiased estimations.

```{r, final model}
model_final <- lme(
  UPDRS ~ Visit + I(Visit^2) + smoking_status + Visit:smoking_status + Age + sex,
  random = ~ Visit | id,
  data = DF,
  method = "ML",
  na.action = na.exclude,
  weights = varExp(form = ~ Visit)
)
```
The results of the model using Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML) showed that there is a significant increase in motor symptom severity (p < 0.001). 

*Visit*
At baseline, the average UPDRS scores increase by 1.47 points per visit (95% CI [0.89,2.05]). This indicates a statistically significant worsening of motor symptoms over time. The positive quadratic term (0.23, 95% CI [0.15, 0.31]) indicates that this rate of increase accelerates over time, consistent with the slight steepening observed in our descriptive analysis. 

*Age*
On average, UPDRS scores increase by 0.25 per a one-year increase in the age of the participant at baseline (95% CI[0.20,0.30]). This indicates that older patients at diagnosis have significantly higher UPDRS scores on average. 

*Sex*
Females have, on average, 0.21 points higher UPDRS scores than males (95% CI[- 1.15,1.56]). There is no statistically significant difference in UPDRS scores between males and females after accounting for our other variables. 

*Smoking*
There is no evidence that smoking history significantly influences either baseline motor symptom severity or the rate of progression over time (Former vs Never baseline: 95% CI [-0.79,2.46]; Current vs Never baseline: 95% CI [-1.40,2.28]; Former progression rate: 95% CI [-0.29,0.76]; Current progression rate: 95% CI [-0.64, 0.55]).

*Heterogeneity*
The between-patient standard deviation of random intercepts is 5.32, which tells us that patients differ substantially in their UPDRS levels at baseline. The between-patient standard deviation of random slopes is 1.82, which tells us that patients also differ in their rate of progression over time.

### Assumptions

Several diagnostic ploys were checked to test the assumptions of the linear mixed-effects model. The residuals vs fitted values plot shows residuals approximately centred around zero with minimal curvature, suggesting the mean structure is aqequately specified. Some residual heteroscedasticity remains in the mid range of fitted values, but this is substantially reduced compared to the model without variance weights, supporting the use of the exponential variance function. The Q-Q plot shows good agreement with normality across most of the distribution, with clear deviation in the upper tail, indicating some departure from normality likely due to a few large positive residuals. Given the large sample size this is not expected to substantially affect the fixed effects estimates, The histogram of random intercepts appears approximately bell-shaped and symmetric, supporting the normality assumption for the random effects.

